[h1]Speculative Markets - Beta[/h1]
The testing channel for Speculative Markets, a Victoria 3 mod about investment, booms, financial panics, and the policies used to manage them.

[b]Enable either this Beta or the stable mod, never both.[/b] Beta is a complete mod, not an add-on. Disable stable manually before enabling Beta.

Changes are tested here before promotion to stable. Use a separate playset and keep copies of saves when trying experimental revisions.

[h2]What's new in 1.2[/h2]
[b]Interest Groups[/b]
Each interest group favors about two goods your country trades and may oppose one. Booms, busts and your interventions in those goods move its approval. See them in the interest group panel and on the Speculation tab.

[b]Monetary Standard[/b]
[b]Bimetallism[/b] makes bank rescues cheaper but booms end harder. The [b]Gold Standard[/b] cuts loan interest and trust losses but makes panics longer. [b]Fiat Money[/b] shortens panics but feeds the bubble and costs trust. Laissez-Faire and Interventionism each turn one into a stronger variant.

[b]Financial Integration[/b]
With Sphere of Influence, power blocs can adopt Financial Integration. Its three levels shelter members from each other's panics, share the leader's Lending Limit and feed the leader's Exchange.

[h2]What's new in 1.1[/h2]
[b]Competing Exchanges[/b]
Start a contest from the Leading Exchanges list or a country's map menu. Standing gives your Exchange one to four contest slots. Each competitor costs influence and strains relations. Each pair settles on its own five-year clock. A lead under 20% is a draw. At 75% or more, a win is decisive. It also attracts a share of the loser's investment pool income for a year if that capital is available.

Bear Raid, Lure Their Capital, Press Campaign and Hold the Line each have a one-year cooldown per competitor. Shore Up halves incoming moves from one competitor and can be renewed after two years. The Competition section shows each contest, the next settlement and the moves running against you.

[b]World Centre for the top three[/b]
The top three Exchanges are World Centres, with more influence, smaller losses from foreign panics and one competitor free of upkeep. The Exchange score now favors size less, so other great powers can compete with the leader.

[b]Paths to Renown and specialities[/b]
Three new charter paths: Futures Pit, Lender to Nations and Railway Mania. Every path now has a drawback that grows with your place, and each pulls levers in other systems as a trade-off. The Renown screen shows rewards on the leader flags and the effects of locked paths.

[b]Blue Chips and Company of the Year[/b]
A company that stays prosperous for a year becomes a blue chip. Each one softens (the first 5 in full, the next 5 half as much) the confidence lost to collapsing booms and foreign panics and pays your Exchange a little trust every year. Events let you cash in a new blue chip or prop up one that slips. Each year the world's most prosperous blue chip is named Company of the Year, with trust and prestige for its country. Each blue chip also gives +0.4% Capitalist investment pool contribution (Blue Chip Index), and moves every contest with a competing Exchange 3% your way. A Bear Raid that lands rattles one of the target's blue chips, so for a year one month below 70 prosperity loses the status.

The Finance tabs are now Trading Floor, Speculation, Banking and Exchanges.

[h2]Latest update[/h2]
[b]1.2.0-beta.1[/b] adds interest group stances, the Monetary Standard and Financial Integration. Tooltips on every tab were rewritten, and the change notes list every balance change. Interest group stances and monetary laws arrive on an existing save at the next monthly update.

[h2]Previous beta update[/h2]
[b]1.1.5-beta.2[/b]
[list]
[*]Banking shows the three biggest contributors to Other Goods, both measures of loan share, and lending conditions when the chart is empty.
[*]Sort goods by their projected hit to market confidence. Recovering goods show their return date, and Great Issues show when the current issue ends and the next can begin.
[*]Panic tooltips show how much lenders called in and whether the investment pool limited the recall. Failed rescue pledges explain what went wrong and show any reserve shortfall.
[*]Company tooltips show when Propped Up and Fragile expire. Charter previews show the trust change and specialities you would lose. Our Last Move now records what you paid to Shore Up and how long protection lasts.
[/list]
On existing saves, new panics, recoveries and company effects record the extra detail as they happen.

